From 7b022d85f7775b0aca1fb1512db5bdd1bf824f53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Edmond <1571649+edmonddantes@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 12:48:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Add ior_release_handle() to take a handle off the
 completion port

A handle stays associated with the port of the context that used it, and
the association belongs to the file object, so a process given the handle
posts the packets of its own overlapped I/O to this port. The packet
filter drops them, but cannot tell one whose OVERLAPPED equals the
address of a slot with a request in flight from that request's own.

ior_release_handle() unties the handle before it is handed out:
NtSetInformationFile(FileReplaceCompletionInformation) with no port,
Windows 8.1 and later. It returns -EBUSY while a read, write, send, recv,
accept or connect of the context on the handle is queued or its
completion not yet dequeued (such an op may issue on the handle again:
a multishot accept's next child, an abort replay): Windows 11 unties a
handle even with a request pending on it, and that request's packet
then reaches no port. -ENOTSUP where ntdll lacks the call or rejects
the class (Wine); -EBADF for NULL or INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE; -EINVAL for a
NULL context or a refusal of the kernel (a closed handle); 0 for a
handle the context does not own, which it leaves as it is. On success
the owner record goes and the handle-set entry is no longer owned, so
the next op on the value associates it again. Other backends tie
nothing to a context and return 0.

test_iocp_backend: after the release another port takes the handle and
a read issued outside ior posts there, not to the ring; the ring's next
write binds it again; a pending read of the ring refuses the release
and still completes; a read pending outside ior is untied with the
handle and completes; a handle never used, or held by another ring, is
left alone.
